      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-11-21T19:04:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Sorry, even though that was the intention, it was not implemented fully but only for pasid table updates(aosp/3786923) and not for the context updates. With the last iteration I pushed,  I have fixed context updates, and added comments as well",
      "parentUuid": "7fa9e945_ec0aa46b",
      "revId": "6ccb3932acedef6becd94e35b4d4d7ba7e59a972",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "04f8c877_335d0f01",
        "filename": "drivers/iommu/intel/iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 35
      },
      "lineNbr": 1476,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-11-26T23:25:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Now looking at the latest version of aosp/3786919, pkvm_iommu_context_addr() may succeed (and thus the hypercall may succeed) but still not update context_gpa to NULL (in case there was already a present context entry for this bus). In such case `ret` is 0, but we should still free this page?\n\nFurthermore, the hypercall may also fail even before calling pkvm_iommu_context_addr(). In such case, context_gpa is not NULL, but we should still free this page?\n\nSo the condition should be `ret || param.context_gpa`?",
      "parentUuid": "dcbeb18e_abf3b32a",
      "revId": "6ccb3932acedef6becd94e35b4d4d7ba7e59a972",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "aed71fdd_232de482",
        "filename": "drivers/iommu/intel/iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 35
      },
      "lineNbr": 1476,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-02T15:00:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Now looking at the latest version of aosp/3786919, pkvm_iommu_context_addr() may succeed (and thus the hypercall may succeed) but still not update context_gpa to NULL (in case there was already a present context entry for this bus). In such case ret is 0, but we should still free this page?\n\nAhh yes, right.\n\n\u003e So the condition should be ret || param.context_gpa?\n\nThere can be cases where the hypercall fails but pkvm_iommu_context_addr() already used this page and set present bit in the root table(in pasid hypercall cl). I thought it would be simpler to leave it as it is as it may be used again and I have seen chances of having more devices in the same context table is really high.\n\nWDYT? If fine with the above, I think we need to only check `param.context_gpa` to free the page. If you feel its worth donating the page back, I shall implement the undonate logic",
      "parentUuid": "04f8c877_335d0f01",
      "revId": "6ccb3932acedef6becd94e35b4d4d7ba7e59a972",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "04573de4_fc7dd9ea",
        "filename": "drivers/iommu/intel/iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 35
      },
      "lineNbr": 1476,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-02T19:08:14Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e There can be cases where the hypercall fails but pkvm_iommu_context_addr() already used this page and set present bit in the root table(in pasid hypercall cl). I thought it would be simpler to leave it as it is as it may be used again and I have seen chances of having more devices in the same context table is really high.\n\nOk, it seems it makes sense to keep it so, for simplicity (and maybe also for consistency with what the host driver normally does).\n\nBut still what about cases when the hypercall fails even before calling pkvm_iommu_context_addr()? Are we fine with a memory leak in the host in such case?\n\nOk, maybe in all such cases it would have already failed in the first pkvm_hc_iommu_set_lm_ce() call, above at line 1470 (with an error different from -ENOMEM), so maybe let\u0027s keep it as is, for simplicity...\n\nIt seems a bit unfortunate that we need to make all these tricky assumptions, instead of making the logic straightforward and easy to reason about (like, for example, in pvVMCS, where we are easily doing graceful undonate/unshare/unpin etc in various cases, in a simple straightforward way), but, well...",
      "parentUuid": "aed71fdd_232de482",
      "revId": "6ccb3932acedef6becd94e35b4d4d7ba7e59a972",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6aca1830_edd38aaf",
        "filename": "drivers/iommu/intel/iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 35
      },
      "lineNbr": 1476,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-02T20:31:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Ok, it seems it makes sense to keep it so, for simplicity (and maybe also for consistency with what the host driver normally does).\n\nyeah host driver also does something similar where the page is not freed once it is used in iommu_context_addr(). Free happens only on iommu deinit.\n\n\u003e But still what about cases when the hypercall fails even before calling pkvm_iommu_context_addr()? Are we fine with a memory leak in the host in such case?\n\nIn this case context_gpa will not be touched and after returning from the hypercall, host driver will free it as it sees context_gpa is non NULL. So effectively I shall move to the first logic where irrespective of `ret`, if `context_gpa` is set, driver will free it. And pkvm will unset it only if it uses the page and sets present bit in root table.\n\n\u003e It seems a bit unfortunate that we need to make all these tricky assumptions, instead of making the logic straightforward and easy to reason about (like, for example, in pvVMCS, where we are easily doing graceful undonate/unshare/unpin etc in various cases, in a simple straightforward way), but, well...\n\nI agree. In 6.18, we plan to move all donations(root, context, pasid and pagetables) via memcache and then host driver need not explicitly donate via context_gpa. If unused, the page will gracefully go back to memcache without host driver needing to do anything explicit.",
      "parentUuid": "04573de4_fc7dd9ea",
      "revId": "6ccb3932acedef6becd94e35b4d4d7ba7e59a972",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "07e0d698_02a96641",
        "filename": "drivers/iommu/intel/iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 35
      },
      "lineNbr": 1476,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-02T21:26:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e In this case context_gpa will not be touched and after returning from the hypercall, host driver will free it as it sees context_gpa is non NULL.\n\nAh, indeed. Seems all good then.\n\n\u003e In 6.18, we plan to move all donations(root, context, pasid and pagetables) via memcache and then host driver need not explicitly donate via context_gpa. If unused, the page will gracefully go back to memcache without host driver needing to do anything explicit.\n\nWhile we\u0027re at it, just thinking how exactly a memcache for pvIOMMU would work; specifically, how and _when_ the host driver would free pages from the memcache. In pvMMU, we are freeing a VM\u0027s memcaches when destroying the VM. In pvIOMMU, it seems the host driver will just _never_ free any pages it allocated for pKVM? IOW once the host driver donated a page to pKVM, no way for the host to reclaim it back? (And that seems ok, since the number of context/pasid/page tables is limited?)\n\n\u003e If unused, the page will gracefully go back to memcache\n\nFYI it may be not that simple. In pvMMU, memcaches are per vCPU, and an unused page doesn\u0027t go back to a memcache, it goes to the per-VM page pool. (And when allocating, pKVM first tries to allocate from the per-VM pool, and only after that from the current vCPU\u0027s memcache.) In pvIOMMU, maybe you can do simpler, but maybe the best way is to do similarly to pvMMU (just s/vCPU/pCPU/ s/per-VM pool/global pool/).",
      "parentUuid": "6aca1830_edd38aaf",
      "revId": "6ccb3932acedef6becd94e35b4d4d7ba7e59a972",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0ec99d00_5bb1204f",
        "filename": "drivers/iommu/intel/iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 35
      },
      "lineNbr": 1476,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-03T16:28:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e While we\u0027re at it, just thinking how exactly a memcache for pvIOMMU would work; specifically, how and when the host driver would free pages from the memcache. In pvMMU, we are freeing a VM\u0027s memcaches when destroying the VM. In pvIOMMU, it seems the host driver will just never free any pages it allocated for pKVM? IOW once the host driver donated a page to pKVM, no way for the host to reclaim it back? (And that seems ok, since the number of context/pasid/page tables is limited?)\n\nI was thinking of donating back to the  host, once a high watermark for number of free pages is reached and a low watermark(could be 0) will trigger the donation from host to pkvm(by failing a hypercall with ENOMEM when it needs pages). \n\n\u003e FYI it may be not that simple. In pvMMU, memcaches are per vCPU, and an unused page doesn\u0027t go back to a memcache, it goes to the per-VM page pool. (And when allocating, pKVM first tries to allocate from the per-VM pool, and only after that from the current vCPU\u0027s memcache.) In pvIOMMU, maybe you can do simpler, but maybe the best way is to do similarly to pvMMU (just s/vCPU/pCPU/ s/per-VM pool/global pool/).\n\nThanks for the explanation, just got a brief understanding of implementation details :-). Yeah we can start with percpu/global pool as you suggested.\n\nJust curious, is the pvmmu memcache designed such a way because there is no synchronous mechanism by which memory could be returned to host? I think pviommu has a more synchronous way to return the pages via hypercall. So probably we might not need a global pool and on every hypercall that could potentially free pages(pasid teardown, iommu_unmap etc), we can check if high watermark is reached and then can return the pages back to host",
      "parentUuid": "07e0d698_02a96641",
      "revId": "6ccb3932acedef6becd94e35b4d4d7ba7e59a972",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fdce8b6c_668ce2a6",
        "filename": "drivers/iommu/intel/iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 35
      },
      "lineNbr": 1476,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-12-03T20:54:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e I was thinking of donating back to the host, once a high watermark for number of free pages\n\nOr maybe no need to donate them back to the host at all? We\u0027d better keep it simple.\n\n\u003e Just curious, is the pvmmu memcache designed such a way because there is no synchronous mechanism by which memory could be returned to host?\n\nYou can ask Quentin why it was designed such a way, but I guess there was simply no real need to introduce extra complexity to support returning those pages back to host earlier than VM teardown (since the amount of pages used for page tables is relatively small and limited). And I suppose it is exactly the same with pvIOMMU?",
      "parentUuid": "0ec99d00_5bb1204f",
      "revId": "6ccb3932acedef6becd94e35b4d4d7ba7e59a972",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
